\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  muffled by weekend lethargy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 1997\par 12:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 14 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shuffled in listless ranges on Friday as the absence of fresh leads reinforced the market's pre-weekend lethargy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading interest was dwindling as banks and investment houses squared their books for the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  "Time is passing a bit slower. It's just the usual year-end phenomenon. People who've made enough money are just trying to protect their positions," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah, on soft ground for much of the day, slid to a low of 3,460 to the dollar on late afternoon rumours concerning Indonesia's PT Bank Central Asia (BCA). \par \par \uc2  The bank's parent Salim Group denied the rumours, which included speculation over the closure of BCA's offices in Singapore, Malaysia and Hong Kong, a clearing deficit in Medan, and the health of the group's co-founder Sudono Salim. \par \par \uc2  Dealers treated the rumours with some skepticism as they occured in thin, easily manipulated Friday afternoon markets. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't be surprised if they're unfounded -- just somebody trying to make extra bucks," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah settled down to 3,435/45 to the dollar by 1030 GMT, little changed from its earlier levels, after the rumours were denied and on talk the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had checked rates or even \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There were some rumours Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in to check rates but the market didn't really sell the dollar down. It's been supported around 3,425/35 all day," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah remained under pressure from dollar demand from domestic companies and Japanese and Hong Kong parties. \par \par \uc2  But players remained cautious about buying the dollar aggressively due to fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have sold dollars above the 3,400 rupiah level early on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Corporate dollar demand also kept the Thai baht weak at 38.85/95 to the dollar onshore against 38.70/38.80 late on Thursday. The offshore rate was at 38.30/40 against 38.30/31. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the initial euphoria over the appointment of Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai had worn off and players were now keenly seeking signs the new government would be committed to economic reform. \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus, in Bangkok as part of a Southeast Asia tour, said Thailand had not sought changes to the terms of its $17.2 billion bailout package. \par \par \uc2  He also said he believed most of Thailand's 58 suspended finance firms were insolvent and should be closed permanently. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off his comments and news Thai foreign reserves had risen at end October due mostly to loan drawdowns from the IMF package. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit remained sandwiched between corporate dollar demand at 3.30 ringgit and dollar offers from agents of Bank Negara at 3.33. \par \par \uc2  Across the border, the Singapore dollar was steady but still weak at 1.5810/25 to the U.S. dollar from 1.5790/00 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "I'd expect dollar/Sing to move higher to 1.5850. There's buying interest from local banks at the lower end and there's still room for competitive depreciation in the Singapore dollar," a Singapore-based analyst said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended lower at 33.99 to the dollar from a previous 33.93 close. Manila traders said it would have weakened further if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not stood firm selling dollars at the 33.98 level. \par \par \uc2  In north Asia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea stuck to its defence of the won, selling dollars at 987.0 won in opening trade and then at 986.00 won in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The won ended at 986.30 to the dollar against Thursday's 986.70 close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won would not breach the key 1,000 level for now but it remained vulnerable to further depreciation. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have inexhaustible dollar reserves to pour into the market. The won's recent strength is a temporary phenomenon," a foreign bank dealer in Seoul said. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ended lower at T$31.240 to the dollar from Thursday's T$31.105 close as investors kept a wary eye on the apparent recovery of the yen and won. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was strong speculative pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the form of non-deliverable forward buying but its falls were cushioned by exporter sales of the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was at 7.7300/20 per U.S. dollar against Thursday's 7.7320/40 close. \par \par \uc2  Interbank rates remained soft as liquidity eased after the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's clarification of rules on borrowing from its discount window. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Secretary for Trade and Industry Denise Yue said she expected the impact of regional market turmoil on Hong Kong to be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbe02k5n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
