\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  muted as won enjoys respite. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 1997\par 06:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked familiar ground on Tuesday as traders waited for the shaky Korean won to slide through a key support level. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in opening trade rescued the won from the brink of the 1,000 to the dollar level, but dealers said its reprieve was likely to be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "The focus is more on the won. There was very aggressive selling (of the dollar) by the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that brought the won back from 999 to 989 but there are still fears about it," the head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The won was at 988.50/9.30 to the dollar at 0450 GMT after firming to 986.0 against an opening at 999.90. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to back its action with strong verbal support also kept traders on guard. \par \par \uc2  "We will absolutely defend the 1,000 won level to the dollar," a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said. "To this end, we will implement stronger measures." \par \par \uc2  The won's recovery boosted Seoul stocks as well with the composite index adding 0.53 percent to 528.12 points. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, the baht slipped to 37.40/60 to the dollar from 37.10/37.30 late on Monday as initial euphoria over the appointment of new Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai faded. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was awaiting details of Chuan's new cabinet and concrete steps to rehabilitate the financial sector. \par \par \uc2  Chuan was quoted in an interview with a Thai newspaper as saying he expected to be able to revitalise confidence in the immediate term to halt capital flight from Thailand and solve liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  "Prime Minister Chuan will have to move fast to capitalise on the positive sentiment the public has for him," a foreign bank dealer in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht's direction would also hinge on the ability of the government and private sector to persuade foreign creditors to roll over $21-$30 billion of short-term debt due by the end of this year. \par \par \uc2  The offshore baht rate also eased to 37.30/60 to the dollar from Monday's 37.20/35 as unwinding of long dollar forward positions abated. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the baht could head higher as the remainder of the positions, established when the baht came under speculative attack six months ago, matured by the end of the week. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah hovered between 3,310 and 3,330 to the dollar, trapped between corporate dollar demand and fears of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the government's pledge on Monday to close 16 unhealthy banks despite potential political fallout had boosted support for the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks of Japan, Singapore and \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the rupiah last week had also helped stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "The 3,350 level is very well resisted and also well monitored at the moment," the Singapore dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit edged up to 3.3035/135 to the dollar from Monday's 3.3150/250, but activity was confined to a very narrow range, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A Standard & Poor's report saying it did not see Malaysia facing the same problems as Indonesia and Thailand helped prop up the ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The S&P report had an impact though not very much. It pushed dollar/ringgit down to 3.30 from around 3.32," another European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso firmed to 33.60/99 to the dollar from Monday's 34.27 close on easing corporate dollar demand and expectations of fund inflows from overseas contract workers. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was also helped by the government's decision to appeal for a reconsideration of last week's Supreme Court decision declaring oil deregulation unconstitutional. \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the Taiwan dollar continued to mirror the won's moves on expectations it would have to undergo a competitive depreciation if the won fell further. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar firmed to T$30.957/999 after slipping to T$31.010/030 against Monday's T$30.985 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was steady at 7.7290/00 to the U.S. dollar while interbank rates remained firm. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbb02phe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
