\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  gain as baht cheers up. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 1997\par 11:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The Thai baht greeted Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh's promise to resign with gusto on Tuesday, bringing cheer to most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The baht made a strong start after Chavalit's surprise announcement late on Monday, sparking predictions that the rally was overdone and would soon fizzle out. \par \par \uc2  But official assurances that political changes would not hurt implementation of IMF-backed economic reforms and news of government spending cuts kept the baht well bid through the day. It ended at its day high of 38.60/80 to the dollar onshore, more than five percent above late Monday levels of 40.90/41.00. The offshore rate was at 38.40/50 at 1040 GMT against 39.85/40.35 previously. \par \par \uc2  "It seems the market is quite receptive to Chavalit's resignation," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  He said spirits were further lifted by announcements that the government would lop another 18 billion baht from 1997/98 spending, on top of a previous 100 billion baht cut. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks also rallied with the composite SET index adding 6.9 percent to end at 478.33. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders remained conservative about the baht's potential for more gains. \par \par \uc2  "I just can't see dollar/baht continuing to come off. I can't see the baht strengthening much further than 37.50/38.00," one said. \par \par \uc2  And while Chavalit's decision to resign removed some of the market's immediate gloom, Thailand still faced a long, arduous road in rehabilitating its economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "A change of leadership obviously means something has to be done and decisions which have to be taken are not going to be popular," said Kim Teo, managing director of Nicholas-Applegate Capital Management Asia. \par \par \uc2  Teo said Southeast Asian markets were in for more pain in the next six to 12 months as Thailand and Indonesia moved to restructure their economies in line with IMF requirements. \par \par \uc2  "The reaction we are seeing in the markets is more a technical bounce from an oversold position," he said. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain weakish but certainly not at the near panic collapses we've seen," he added. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah continued to rise on appreciation of the government's efforts to reform the economy in response to a massive IMF-led international aid package. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the scramble caused by tighter liquidity after three central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to buy the rupiah lifted forward rates in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The value of concerted dollar sales for rupiah in Singapore on Monday by the Bank of Japan (BOJ), the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) and Bank Indonesia was estimated at $300 to $500 million, senior Japanese traders said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore dealers said Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Tuesday to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  "They sold quite a few times even around the low of 3,210. I don't think it was really big amounts but they tried to cap (the dollar) above 3,270," the U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was at 3,260/70 to the dollar against its New York close at 3,205, sharply up from its Monday Asian close at 3,300/10. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5555/75 to the U.S. dollar, off its highs of 1.5450 after active U.S. dollar buying by local banks below the 1.5500 level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were rumours the dollar purchases were on behalf of the MAS, which was believed to be buying back dollars after Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's ringgit was at 3.2675/775 to the dollar against 3.2600/700 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said selling was expected after the ringgit's recent bounce, but they saw a further test of the 3.20 per dollar resistance. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended higher at 34.84 to the dollar from a previous 35.14 close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered dollars at 34.85/90 pesos but found no takers. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gabriel Singson said the policy-making Monetary Board would discuss by how much to lower the overnight lending rate at a regular meeting on Wednesday. The rate is at 16.125 percent. \par \par \uc2  Even the Taiwan dollar and South Korean won perked up on Tuesday. The Taiwan dollar ended sharply up at T$30.730 against Monday's 10-year low of T$31.245. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said investors took profit on U.S. dollar gains after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday the Taiwan dollar had lost too much value. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the Taiwan dollar's recent depreciation made Taiwan remained "extremely competitive" against South Korea and Singapore in global export markets. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended at 961.00 against Monday's 969.90 close after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poured an estimated $300 million into the market to support it. \par \par \uc2  Only the Hong Kong dollar slipped as traders unwound long positions on a belief that the premium of Hong Kong dollar interest rates over U.S. rates would wane. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said last week that domestic interest rates were likely to ease in the short term. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb4037tk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
