\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  leap as optimism returns. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 1,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 1997\par 11:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 3 (Reuters) - Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  leapt on Monday as Indonesia's early efforts to reform its economy in response to a massive IMF-led aid package sparked hopes the region might be headed for a turnaround. \par \par \uc2  Asian stock markets, particularly Hong Kong and Singapore, also benefitted from the rosier mood, rising substantially. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah led Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, shooting to a high of 3,240 to the dollar, a gain of more than nine percent from its opening around 3,540/55 after Japan and Singapore said they had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In separate statements released in Singapore, the two Finance Ministries said the Bank of Japan (BOJ) and \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Singapore Finance Minister Richard Hu said in a statement the MAS bought Indonesian rupiah against the U.S. dollar in concert with the other authorities. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore's participation in this joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflects our confidence in the macroeconomic policies of the Indonesian government," it said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "aimed at correcting excessive depreciation of the Indonesian rupiah," and the three authorities would be ready to act again when necessary. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupiah, which opened on an upbeat note, sharply extended its gains after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was confirmed. \par \par \uc2  "The market was already very happy with the package...It was very timely to come in and strike while the iron was hot," said a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  She said Japan and Singapore had probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in "token amounts" with \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling the bulk of dollars, but the move caught players off-guard in a market severely thinned by a Japanese holiday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore and Japan said on Saturday they were each ready to extend US$5.0 billion to Indonesia to supplement resources organised by the International Monetary Fund (IMF). \par \par \uc2  Indonesia could receive upto US$40 billion in financial aid from multilateral agencies and individual countries provided it meets strict IMF conditions to rehabilitate its economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts saw Monday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as aimed at curbing a further loss of confidence in Indonesia, especially in the face of Thailand's persistent woes. \par \par \uc2  "I guess regional central banks felt they could have done more in the case of Thailand. They didn't and things got worse, so if they didn't do anything aggressive now, they'd never turn it around," said Chia Woon Khien, head of Asian research at Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Indonesia's case was also slightly less problematic so they were more willing to commit their money and the market was more willing to believe in their commitment," she added. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian government liquidation of 16 unhealthy banks, some of them linked to President Suharto's family, was welcomed by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets. \par \par \uc2  "Indonesia has helped a lot. They've taken firm actions, unlike the Thais," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  In August, the IMF organised $17.2 billion in aid for Thailand and on Monday the Thai baht was the only Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall, plagued by political uncertainties and delays in implementing financial sector reforms. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 40.90/41.000 to the dollar onshore at 1005 GMT, off a low of 41.00 against Friday's 40.70/41.00. It was firmer offshore at 40.20/40 against 40.60/90 previously. \par \par \uc2  Thai Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh came under fresh pressure to resign on Monday as a key coalition partner, former prime minister Chatichai Choonhavan, quit his advisory post. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off expressions of confidence in the baht's long-term stability fromFinance Minister Kosit Panpiemras, whot urged Thais to refrain from buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless absolutely necessary. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar remained near its highs at 1.5525/55 to the U.S. dollar against an opening below 1.5740. Singapore's benchmark stock index ended the day with a hefty 7.83 percent rise to 1,703.95 points. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like dollar/Sing has seen a top at 1.5970. If it rallies to 1.5700/40 now, I'd favour a sell," another European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit surged to a high of 3.2500 against 3.4070/180 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Comments by U.S. financier George Soros also fuelled buying of Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. He told the British Broadcasting Corporation (BBC) he believed the world's financial turbulence was over and called for international regulatory coordination to head off further storms. \par \par \uc2  "What he says goes," said the U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended up at 35.14 to the dollar against Friday's 35.42 close on gains in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell $500,000. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gabriel Singson said he would meet commercial bank presidents to discuss ways to cut bank lending rates and reduce volatility in the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar ploughed new depths, ending at T$31.245 to the U.S. dollar amid widespread expectations of long-term depreciation as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained out of view. \par \par \uc2  After the market closed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cited an external index showing the Taiwan dollar had lost too much value, but vowed to let the market determine its exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won ended weak at 969.9 to the dollar against 964.60 on Friday despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A Bank of Korea official told Reuters on Saturday the battered won would "never, never, never" breach the psychological 1,000 to the dollar level. \par \par \uc2  "That was a dangerous statement. It immediately made the won go to 970 today: never say never," said a Singapore dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar received a boost from a firm set of retail sales data, swinging sentiment against a rate cut. It firmed to $0.7129/34 from an early $0.7007/12. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb303efl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
