\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Rupiah shaky despite BI move. \b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 6 (Reuter) - The Indonesian rupiah remained badly shaken on Monday after its steep early drop drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell dollars for the first time in weeks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah dived to an all-time low of 3,845 to the dollar as domestic companies and offshore funds scrambled for dollars, bringing down other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well. \par \par \uc2  It was back at 3,800 at 0445 GMT after recovering to around 3,725 on Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with an opening around 3,700/50. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not sell a large amount of dollars, as the market was already very thin with deals of $1-$2 million triggering wild swings. \par \par \uc2  The unexpected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave the rupiah some respite but dealers said its prospects remained grim, with seeing it headed for 4,000 to the dollar as Indonesian companies grappled with huge foreign debts. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people don't see an end to this. It's now like a chicken and egg situation. Companies that were fine at 2,500 (rupiah to the dollar) are not at 3,800," said a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  She said there were growing rumours that Indonesian companies with large dollar call options were going to default on their positions. \par \par \uc2  "Many of them have written dollar calls and they're not professional option traders. So they've left them unhedged," she said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit succumbed to fresh selling as players quickly shrugged off news of a large trade surplus in August and looked for excuses to buy the dollar again. It was at 3.3730/800 to the dollar against an early high of 3.3580. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia announced an August trade surplus of 1.2 billion ringgit on Saturday, after deficits of 900 million ringgit in July and 2.8 billion ringgit in June. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's repeated calls for tight curbs on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading kept a lid on the ringgit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Mahathir told a news conference in Buenos Aires on Friday that current regulation was clearly insufficient. \par \par \uc2  "We have already set a limit to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading...but it is quite obvious that it is not effective," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected dollar/ringgit to stay firm but its rise could be limited by caution ahead of Malaysia's budget on October 17. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar looked set to head for 1.55 to the U.S. dollar as buying of the ringgit from opening levels of 45.60/65 Singapore cents depressed the Singapore unit, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was at 1.5485/95 against 1.5422/37 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened along with its neighbours as players turned to the dollar ahead of Thailand's eagerly awaited financial reform package, due on October 15. \par \par \uc2  News at the weekend that Thailand might need more than the $17.2 billion loan package arranged by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) to pull out of its economic crisis also depressed the baht, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  It fell to 36.80/37.00 per dollar onshore against 36.40/36.50 late on Friday. It was at 36.30/45 offshore against 35.55/75. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand Assistant Governor Siri Ganjarerndee told a conference in Bangkok that Thailand's original assumptions about the amount of short-term debt to be rolled over may have been overly optimistic. \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, surging overnight money rates kept the peso somewhat protected from the region's woes, but dealers said it showed signs of weakness. \par \par \uc2  The interbank call loan rate hit a high of 105 percent, knocking the wind out of the stock market where the main index breached its 2,000 support to end down 3.37 percent at 1,961.24. \par \par \uc2  The peso was at 34.926 to the dollar against Friday's 34.91 close, reflecting last week's "gentlemen's agreement" among bankers not to trade the peso below 34.926 to the dollar and to limit over-the-counter dollar sales to the 35.275 rate. \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Anchalee Koetsawang in Bangkok (662) 652-0642, Yoo Choon-Sik in Seoul (822) 727 5646, Lilian Kurunungan in Manila (632-841-8938) and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dta601ist\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
