\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Rupiah Plunges Again; IMF Help May Be Needed \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jay Solomon \par \uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 1997\par 13:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--Indonesia's rupiah plunged again Monday, prompting some economists to suggest the country may need to turn to the International Monetary Fund and World Bank to restore lasting stability and confidence to the country's financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah fell to another record low of 3,857.50 rupiah to the dollar Monday before before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a meeting of top economic officials hosted by President Suharto helped to reverse the slide somewhat. \par \par \uc2  Even so, the dollar was trading late Monday at 3,717.50 rupiah, up from 3,660 rupiah late Friday and 3,405 rupiah late on Thursday - losing 8.4% of its value in two trading sessions. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dropped 37% since the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  The meeting at the presidential palace resulted in new measures to help domestic exporters get access to imports. And Coordinating Minister for Economy, Finance, and Development Supervision Saleh Afiff announced that the government is lobbying international creditors to roll over their lendings to Indonesian companies to ease the pressure on the rupiah. State Secretary Moerdiono issued a plea to local companies not to hoard their dollar holdings. \par \par \uc2  Some Indonesian companies have been struggling to repay dollar borrowings taken out before the recent collapse in the rupiah's value. \par \par \uc2  But several economists argue that domestic measures won't be enough. \par \par \uc2  'There's an enormous shortage of dollars (among Indonesian companies) but it's also a psychological problem - that is why the IMF or World Bank are needed,' said Kwik Kian Gie, a prominent Indonesian economist. 'No one believes that the government alone can solve the problem.' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 06-10-97 \par \par \uc2  1106GMT \par \par \uc2  1139 4204 62554 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dta607hgl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
