\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia/2,900 Rupiah -2: New All-Time Low Below \b0\par\par\uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 1997\par 08:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indonesian rupiah has fallen to an all-time below 2,900 rupiah Friday as many Indonesian interests - caught short of U.S. dollars when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  floated the rupiah Thursday - are continuing to cover short positions, dealers in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also say that speculators offshore are continuing to test the limit that Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the rupiah to fall before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Soedradjad Djiwandono said Thursday that he wouldn't refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in order to keep the rupiah's value 'stable.' \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is now trading at 2,925 rupiah at 0601 GMT (2:01 a.m. EDT), up a half a percentage point from Thursday's close of 2,785 rupiah. Dealers say they expect the dollar to continue trading higher. \par \par \uc2  'There were many locals who thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to try and keep the rupiah within the (12%) band,' said Pardi Kandy, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at PT Mitsubishi Bank Buana in Jakarta. 'When the rupiah was floated, they were caught short (dollars).' \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-08-97 \par \par \uc2  (Corrected at 0228GMT) \par \par \uc2  0623GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt8f061qk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
