\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia adopts more flexible exchange rate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1997\par 12:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuter) - Bank Indonesia kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on its toes on Thursday by announcing it would stop publishing formal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits for the rupiah, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into uncharted territory. \par \par \uc2  While the move had been anticipated by many in the market, the timing came as a surprise given the Indonesian authorities on Wednesday raised interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the rupiah breaching the bottom of the now defunct band. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were positive about the move and, given the recent turmoil and period of economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment in the region, said it made perfect sense to allow more exchange rate flexibility. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sudradjad Djiwandono said on Thursday the bank's decision was taken because other countries in the region had floated their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Indonesia is the only one with a band," he said. "It's no longer an advantage." \par \par \uc2  Djiwandono said the lifting of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band implied a free float of the rupiah although it reserved the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed. \par \par \uc2  "Theoretically, it's a free float," he told Reuters Financial Television in an interview. "But of course Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep developments on course, to smooth the adaptment period." \par \par \uc2  However, analysts very much doubted that it signalled a pure free float and while Bank Indonesia may not publish levels, it almost certainly had them in mind. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is totally out of the market so it's not a real free float in that sense," said Chiang Yao Chye, Head of Asia Pacific Research at CIBC in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "It's obviously some kind of managed float...it's very likely that they do have a band in mind but the big difference is that it's not going to be as transparent as before and set targets for speculators to aim at." \par \par \uc2  The rupiah finally settled just shy of the 2,800 level in late business on Thursday, about five percent down on the day. \par \par \uc2  A U.S. bank dealer in Jakarta said offshore players drove the action although overall volume was light. \par \par \uc2  In late trading on Wednesday the rupiah sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by inching down to flirt with the old lower band limit of 2,682. \par \par \uc2  "I think to move to the current level is a bit difficult to explain on the basis of fundamentals but we haven't seen much of a focus on fundamentals in any of these markets in the past few weeks," said Kobus Van Der Wath, Regional Head of Treasury Economics at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  Lack of liquidity has had a lot to do with the sort of wild \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves seen of late, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The move would seem to be similar to that adopted by Thailand after the baht crisis. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used to publish a daily rate for the baht against the dollar and it was kept within a four satang range around that level, in other words a band in which the baht would be kept. \par \par \uc2  After the crisis, the bank continued to publish a daily reference rate but did not have an official range. \par \par \uc2  "If there had been a band widening it would not have worked because...you are exposed in the light and the speculators are hidden in the dark," said the then Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rerngchai Marakanond. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia is thinking along the same lines and has simply stopped publishing the rates, although the move still signals a more flexible approach to exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "Initially (after the baht crisis) central banks slowly tried to regain control but the thinking now appears to be embracing the concept of flexible exchange rates with the hope of deriving the benefits of lower interest rates and perhaps more relaxed forward curves," Standard Chartered's Van Der Wath said. \par \par \uc2  The question which was now being asked in the market was whether Indonesia would go the way of Malaysia and introduce some kind of mild capital controls as a partial counter to the more flexible system. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia recently adopted a system similar to that in Singapore which prevents sizeable non-trade forward trades. \par \par \uc2  By imposing these controls overseas players are limited in the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they can obtain, thereby limiting the scope for speculation. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it's Bank Indonesia's style," said Van Der Wath. "It would take the edge off speculators' activities and is a possibility, but not really Bank Indonesia's style." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8e019p1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
