\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia, Forced To Float Rupiah, Bracing For Corporate Impact \b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1997\par 18:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- Indonesia was finally forced by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators Thursday to join several of its Southeast Asian neighbors and float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolished the 12% dollar-rupiah trading band, and allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float. The move - ending what has long been seen as Indonesia's "free lunch" - sent the rupiah plunging nearly 7%, and caused the Jakarta Stock Exchange to lose a further 16 points on the day. \par \par \uc2  From a pre-float level of 2,640 rupiah, the U.S. dollar shot up almost immediately to 2,755 rupiah. The dollar then traded slightly down, before ending Asian trading at 2,825 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators had fixed their sights on \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , only to be stopped when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  - with massive trades to buy rupiah - to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Indonesia Governor Soedradjad Djiwandono conceded Indonesia's previous "managed depreciation" policy become a liability amid the new era of liberalized \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes. \par \par \uc2  "Our managed band was once seen as liberal, and an advantage for Indonesia," Soedradjad said. "But with Thailand and the Philippines floating, it no longer is." \par \par \uc2  Central banks around the region have been signaling they aren't prepared to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a surging U.S. dollar; the Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit led regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower Tuesday on new indications of this policy, though both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more stable Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  For years, the rupiah's high yields, combined with the predictability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's managed depreciation policy, offered incredibly high returns for investors - with little risk. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained strong despite numerous political and economic shocks within the county. \par \par \uc2  "I just think we couldn't believe this would ever end," said the president-director of a European brokerage house in Jakarta. "Obviously, we were wrong." \par \par \uc2  Economists and financial analysts lauded the Bank Indonesia's "prudent action," but there's a growing chorus of concern over issues ranging from the the impact of the free-float on Indonesian companies to the likelihood of increased inflation. \par \par \uc2  Significantly exposed to offshore debt - and believed to be insufficiently hedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses - commerical balance sheets are expected to suffer severe damage from the rupiah's current level. \par \par \uc2  "You hear many corporates saying they are properly hedged against the rupiah's plunge. You hear them say that their bottom lines aren't going to be affected," said Agnes Safford, president-director of ABN Amro Hoare Govett in Jakarta. "I wish it were true, but it is hard to fathom." \par \par \uc2  Pharmaceuticals, electronics, and electrical appliance companies are expected to particularly hard hit, analysts say, and large companies, such as HM Sampoerna, PT Bakrie & Brothers, and the Sinar Mas Group, will feel the impact. \par \par \uc2  With corporate earnings in serious doubt, the stock market forecast isn't particularly rosy. Analysts say foreign investors are hardly expected to jump back into the equities markets, although fixed-income investments will continue to look attractive with the Indonesian economy unsettled. Late Thursday, an overnight rupiah time deposit was yielding a heady 28%. \par \par \uc2  In just over a week, Jakarta's main equity index has dropped 10% to Thursday's close of 643.011. It's down 15% since hitting an all-time high of 740 on July 8. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad said Thursday that the liquidity squeeze is "only temporary" and market participants believe him. They also note, however, that with inflation expected to rise on the back of the rupiah's depreciation - and foreign inflows a must - the extent to which Bank Indonesia can reasonably cut rates is limited. The concern is that the current situation will expose greater cracks in the nation's financial system, than any previously expected. \par \par \uc2  Indonesian banks, for example, are not known for the quality of their assets. Any serious cash-flow problems experienced by a major Indonesian conglomerate could have a ripple effect throughout the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We think for the time being that the banking sector looks relatively okay," said an official at an international financial organization in Jakarta. "But it's in an environment like this one that you realize you have problems that you weren't aware of before." \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1198 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dt8e00nio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
