\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Commodity Markets Shrug Off Rupiah Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1997\par 13:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's key commodity markets were relatively undisturbed by the rupiah's break out of its trading band on continued selling pressure against the dollar Wednesday, traders from a variety of commodity market sectors said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  For a short period mid-afternoon in Asia, the rupiah traded outside its 2,378-2,682 rupiah band against the dollar. Around 0600 GMT, it traded at 2,685 rupiah, before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helped it to stage a recovery to its current 2,665 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness didn't take traders by surprise - it had been falling all week - it generated keen selling interest from Indonesian rubber exporters hoping to obtain nominally higher returns in rupiah from selling Standard Indonesian Rubber 20 in the dollar-denominated market. \par \par \uc2  However, dealings were unspectacular overall, traders said, as buyers retreated on expectations that the rupiah could fall further. Few deals were completed. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia is the world's second-largest producer of rubber, after Thailand. According to the U.K.-based International Rubber Study Group, in 1996 it produced 1.54 million metric tons and is expected to produce 1.65 million tons this year. \par \par \uc2  The trading pattern was similar in the palm oil market, where the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  generated increased buying interest, but little in the way of completed business. \par \par \uc2  While price action in the cash palm oil market was lethargic, prices on the Kuala Lumpur Commodity Exchange, boosted by the similar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  woes of Malaysia's ringgit, closed at their highs. \par \par \uc2  However, while the KLCE benchmark October contract closed at a high of 1,250 ringgit a ton, the cash price of Indonesian crude palm oil dropped about 15 rupiah a kilogram to 1,230-1,240 rupiah at the close. Indonesian palm oil product prices were unchanged on the day. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia is the world's second-largest palm oil producer, after Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES COMMODITIES SERVICE 13-08-97 \par \par \uc2  1139GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020011006dt8d00woy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
