\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Indonesia's Defense Of Embattled Rupiah Seems Futile \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jay Solomon \par \uc2  Staff Reporter \par \uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1997\par 17:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators Wednesday pushed the Indonesian rupiah through the weak end of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band and sparked numerous - though unconfirmed - reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent upwards of $200 million \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defense of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This apparent action did succeed in pushing the rupiah back within its 12% band, but traders in Singapore and Jakarta remain convinced an eventual second attack on the rupiah's band is inevitable. \par \par \uc2  Many economists assert a defense of the rupiah is justified on grounds it's undervalued, but also concede the speculative fervor currently in the market likely makes such a defense futile. \par \par \uc2  "The Indonesian authorities are in a very difficult situation with no real answers," said Don Hanna, a regional economist at Goldman Sachs & Co. in Hong Kong. "We certainly believe the rupiah is fairly valued within its current band. But at the same time, we don't want to see Indonesia falling on its own sword defending it." \par \par \uc2  Pressure on the rupiah has been felt all week on the back of continued speculative attacks against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a strengthening of the dollar globally. \par \par \uc2  But early Wednesday, the attack intensified, as traders at offshore banks began selling the rupiah for dollars as rumors swept the Singapore market that Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was preparing to abandon its current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band in favor of a freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Indonesian companies were also seen buying dollars to hedge against rupiah exposure. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia responded by raising the rates of its Sertifikat Bank Indonesia, or SBI, money-market paper across the board by a full percentage point. But little support was seen. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market grew bolder hours later on news Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and finance minister had scheduled a lunch-time news conference. Speculation centered on the possible announcement of a free-floating policy, but the meeting turned out to be a tutorial for the local media on coverage of the rupiah's woes. The foreign press was barred from the meeting. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Soedradjad Djiwandono instructed the chief editors of local papers to be less alarmist and speculative in their reporting, according to those in attendance. But in a comment that could be perceived as a tip-off, he also said the government "would use all of its monetary tools" to stabilize the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At around the same time, the dollar penetrated the top of its trading band against the rupiah, reaching a high of 2,682 rupiah. It was the first time the top end of the band has been broken during the recent spate of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attacks. \par \par \uc2  Just a half an hour later, however, the dollar plunged to an intraday low of 2,630 rupiah on what traders said was a massive Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , involving the sale of around $200 million. Other traders believe that Bank Indonesia spent as much as $500 million. \par \par \uc2  The dollar gradually began to trend upward to the weak end of the rupiah band, which was again set by Bank Indonesia at 2,378 rupiah to 2,682 rupiah. The dollar closed Asian trading Thursday at 2,670 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's quick rebound after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  supports the belief of many market participants that another run on the rupiah is imminent. \par \par \uc2  They note that Indonesia is now the only member of the Association of Southeast Asian Nation's "Big Four" - which includes Thailand, Malaysia, and the Philippines - that hasn't moved to a freely-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This view holds that a further deprecation of the rupiah is necessary to keep Indonesia in line with its regional competitors. \par \par \uc2  But many economists differ, asserting that Indonesia's economy is actually much sounder on a fundamental basis than these three neighbors. This strength is particularly evident in recent trends in the balance of trade and inflation. For example,  Indonesia last week registered its strongest performance in trade and inflation for the year. Indonesia's May trade surplus totaled $970 million, and non-oil exports - long a source of concern for economists - grew 13% to a record $3.61 billion. \par \par \uc2  At the present rate, Indonesia's current-account deficit is seen improving upon the 4% of gross domestic product projected earlier in the year. Annual inflation is poised to improve upon the 6.5% registered last year. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's fiscal and monetary management has also been widely lauded. Economist note that Bank Indonesia is the only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Asean four preemptively to widen it \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band in anticipation of recent turmoil. \par \par \uc2  In light of these strong fundamentals, many economists believe a defense of the rupiah may be justified. They note that if Bank Indonesia is forced to float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market may distort the rupiah's true value, sending inflation and debt-servicing costs higher. \par \par \uc2  "In the current environment you could see the market undercut the rupiah's true value by 12% to 15%," said William Belchere, the Singapore head of fixed-income research at Merrill Lynch. "In this case, would I say an attempt to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is worthwhile? Yes." \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1194 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dt8d00m8a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
